#show: template.with(
  title: "Mathematical Creation",
  author: "Henri Poincaré", date: "1908", lang: "en",
  css: ("/assets/custom.css", "/assets/essay.css"),
)

= #text("Mathematical Creation / 数学创造")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("The genesis of mathematical creation is a problem which should intensely interest the psychologist. It is the activity in which the human mind seems to take least from the outside world, in which it acts or seems to act only of itself and on itself, so that in studying the procedure of geometric thought we may hope to reach what is most essential in man's mind.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("数学创造的起源是一个应该引起心理学家强烈兴趣的问题。在这种活动中，人类的思维似乎从外部世界获取的东西最少，在这种活动中，人类思维仅凭自身或自身起作用，因此，在研究几何思维的过程时，我们可能希望了解人类思维中最本质的东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("This has long been appreciated, and some time back the journal called L'enseignement mathématique, edited by Laisant and Fehr, began an investigation of the mental habits and methods of work of different mathematicians. I had finished the main outlines of this article when the results of that inquiry were published, so I have hardly been able to utilize them and shall confine myself to saying that the majority of witnesses confirm my conclusions; I do not say all, for when the appeal is to universal suffrage unanimity is not to be hoped.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("人们很早就认识到这一点，不久前，由莱桑特和费尔编辑的《数学研究》杂志开始对不同数学家的心理习惯和工作方法进行调查。当调查结果发表时，我已经完成了本文的主要提纲，因此我几乎无法使用它们，只能说大多数证人证实了我的结论；我并没有说出全部，因为当呼吁普选时，就不能指望获得一致通过。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("A first fact should surprise us, or rather would surprise us if we were not so used to it. How does it happen there are people who do not understand mathematics? If mathematics invokes only the rules of logic, such as are accepted by all normal minds; if its evidence is based on principles common to all men, and that none could deny without being mad, how does it come about that so many persons are here refractory?")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("第一个事实应该让我们感到惊讶，或者如果我们不那么习惯的话，更确切地说，会让我们感到惊讶。怎么会有不懂数学的人呢？如果数学仅引用逻辑规则，例如所有正常人都接受的规则；如果它的证据是基于所有人共同的原则，而且没有人会在不发疯的情况下否认这一点，那么为什么这里有这么多人不听话呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("That not every one can invent is nowise mysterious. That not every one can retain a demonstration once learned may also pass. But that not every one can understand mathematical reasoning when explained appears very surprising when we think of it. And yet those who can follow this reasoning only with difficulty are in the majority: that is undeniable, and will surely not be gainsaid by the experience of secondary-school teachers.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("并不是每个人都能发明，这一点也不神秘。并非每个人都能记住一次学会的演示，这一点也可能会过去。但当我们想到这一点时，并不是每个人都能理解数学推理的解释，这就显得非常令人惊讶。然而，能够遵循这一推理的人却是大多数：这是不可否认的，也决不会被中学教师的经验所否定。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("And further: how is error possible in mathematics? A sane mind should not be guilty of a logical fallacy, and yet there are very fine minds who do not trip in brief reasoning such as occurs in the ordinary doings of life, and who are incapable of following or repeating without error the mathematical demonstrations which are longer, but which after all are only an accumulation of brief reasonings wholly analogous to those they make so easily. Need we add that mathematicians themselves are not infallible?")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("更进一步：数学中怎么可能出现错误？理智的头脑不应该犯逻辑谬误，然而，有一些非常优秀的头脑，他们不会在日常生活中发生的简短推理中绊倒，也无法毫无错误地遵循或重复较长的数学证明，但这些证明毕竟只是与他们如此容易做出的那些完全相似的简短推理的积累。我们需要补充一点，数学家本身并不是绝对正确的吗？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("The answer seems to me evident. Imagine a long series of syllogisms, and that the conclusions of the first serve as premises of the following: we shall be able to catch each of these syllogisms, and it is not in passing from premises to conclusion that we are in danger of deceiving ourselves. But between the moment in which we first meet a proposition as conclusion of one syllogism, and that in which we reencounter it as premise of another syllogism occasionally some time will elapse, several links of the chain will have unrolled; so it may happen that we have forgotten it, or worse, that we have forgotten its meaning. So it may happen that we replace it by a slightly different proposition, or that, while retaining the same enunciation, we attribute to it a slightly different meaning, and thus it is that we are exposed to error.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("在我看来，答案是显而易见的。想象一长串三段论，第一个三段论的结论充当以下的前提：我们将能够抓住这些三段论中的每一个，并且从前提到结论的过程中我们不会面临欺骗自己的危险。但是，从我们第一次遇到作为一个三段论的结论的命题到我们再次遇到它作为另一个三段论的前提的那一刻之间，有时会经过一段时间，链条上的几个环节就会展开；因此，我们可能会忘记它，或者更糟糕的是，我们忘记了它的含义。因此，我们可能会用一个稍微不同的命题来替换它，或者在保留相同的表述的同时，我们赋予它一个稍微不同的含义，因此我们就会犯错误。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("Often the mathematician uses a rule. Naturally he begins by demonstrating this rule; and at the time when this proof is fresh in his memory he understands perfectly its meaning and its bearing, and he is in no danger of changing it. But subsequently he trusts his memory and afterward only applies it in a mechanical way; and then if his memory fails him, he may apply it all wrong. Thus it is, to take a simple example, that we sometimes make slips in calculation because we have forgotten our multiplication table.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("数学家经常使用规则。自然地，他首先展示了这条规则；当这个证明在他的记忆中清晰可见时，他就完全理解了它的意义和意义，并且他没有改变它的危险。但后来他相信了自己的记忆，然后只是机械地运用它；如果他记不住，他可能会完全错误地应用它。因此，举一个简单的例子，我们有时会因为忘记了乘法表而在计算中犯错误。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("According to this, the special aptitude for mathematics would be due only to a very sure memory or to a prodigious force of attention. It would be a power like that of the whist-player who remembers the cards played; or, to go up a step, like that of the chess-player who can visualize a great number of combinations and hold them in his memory. Every good mathematician ought to be a good chess-player, and inversely; likewise he should be a good computer. Of course that sometimes happens; thus Gauss was at the same time a geometer of genius and a very precocious and accurate computer.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("据此，数学的特殊天赋只能归因于非常可靠的记忆力或惊人的注意力。这将是一种像惠斯特玩家一样的力量，他能记住所打的牌。或者，更上一层楼，就像国际象棋棋手一样，他可以想象出大量的组合并将它们牢记在心。每个优秀的数学家都应该是一名优秀的国际象棋棋手，反之亦然；同样，他应该是一台好的计算机。当然有时会发生这种情况；因此，高斯同时是一位天才的几何学家和一台非常早熟且精确的计算机。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("But there are exceptions; or rather I err; I can not call them exceptions without the exceptions being more than the rule. Gauss it is, on the contrary, who was an exception. As for myself, I must confess, I am absolutely incapable even of adding without mistakes. In the same way I should be but a poor chess-player; I would perceive that by a certain play I should expose myself to a certain danger; I would pass in review several other plays, rejecting them for other reasons, and then finally I should make the move first examined, having meantime forgotten the danger I had foreseen.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("但也有例外；或者说我错了；如果例外超出了规则，我就不能称它们为例外。相反，高斯是个例外。至于我自己，我必须承认，我根本无法做到无错误地添加。同样的，我也应该是一个蹩脚的棋手；我会意识到，通过某种游戏，我应该让自己面临某种危险；我会通过审查其他几个剧本，因其他原因拒绝它们，然后最后我应该首先审查这一举动，同时忘记了我预见到的危险。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("In a word, my memory is not bad, but it would be insufficient to make me a good chess-player. Why then does it not fail me in a difficult piece of mathematical reasoning where most chess-players would lose themselves? Evidently because it is guided by the general march of the reasoning. A mathematical demonstration is not a simple juxtaposition of syllogisms, it is syllogisms placed in a certain order, and the order in which these elements are placed is much more important than the elements themselves. If I have the feeling, the intuition, so to speak, of this order, so as to perceive at a glance the reasoning as a whole, I need no longer fear lest I forget one of the elements, for each of them will take its allotted place in the array, and that without any effort of memory on my part.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("总而言之，我的记忆力还不错，但还不足以使我成为一名优秀的棋手。那么，为什么在大多数棋手都会迷失方向的困难数学推理中，它没有让我失望呢？显然是因为它是由推理的总体进程引导的。数学证明不是三段论的简单并置，而是按一定顺序放置的三段论，而这些元素放置的顺序比元素本身重要得多。可以说，如果我对这个顺序有感觉，有直觉，以便一眼就能看出整个推理，我就不再担心忘记其中一个元素，因为它们中的每一个都会在数组中占据其分配的位置，而我不需要任何记忆力。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("It seems to me then, in repeating a reasoning learned, that I could have invented it. This is often only an illusion; but even then, even if I am not so gifted as to create it by myself, I myself re-invent it in so far as I repeat it.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("在我看来，通过重复学到的推理，我可以发明它。这往往只是一种幻觉；但即便如此，即使我没有那么有天赋能够自己创造它，我自己也会在重复它的过程中重新发明它。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("We know that this feeling, this intuition of mathematical order, that makes us divine hidden harmonies and relations, can not be possessed by every one. Some will not have either this delicate feeling so difficult to define, or a strength of memory and attention beyond the ordinary, and then they will be absolutely incapable of understanding higher mathematics. Such are the majority. Others will have this feeling only in a slight degree, but they will be gifted with an uncommon memory and a great power of attention. They will learn by heart the details one after another; they can understand mathematics and sometimes make applications, but they cannot create. Others, finally, will possess in a less or greater degree the special intuition referred to, and then not only can they understand mathematics even if their memory is nothing extraordinary, but they may become creators and try to invent with more or less success according as this intuition is more or less developed in them.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("我们知道，这种感觉，这种数学秩序的直觉，使我们拥有神圣的隐藏和谐和关系，并不是每个人都拥有。有些人要么没有这种难以定义的微妙感觉，要么没有超凡的记忆力和注意力，那么他们就绝对无法理解高等数学。这是大多数。其他人只会有轻微的这种感觉，但他们会被赋予不寻常的记忆力和强大的注意力。他们会一个接一个地记住细节；他们可以理解数学，有时也可以进行应用，但他们无法创造。最后，其他人将或多或少地拥有所提到的特殊直觉，然后他们不仅可以理解数学，即使他们的记忆力并不特别，而且他们可能成为创造者，并根据这种直觉在他们身上或多或少的发展而尝试或多或少地成功发明。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("In fact, what is mathematical creation? It does not consist in making new combinations with mathematical entities already known. Any one could do that, but the combinations so made would be infinite in number and most of them absolutely without interest. To create consists precisely in not making useless combinations and in making those which are useful and which are only a small minority. Invention is discernment, choice.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("事实上，什么是数学创造？它不在于与已知的数学实体进行新的组合。任何人都可以做到这一点，但这样做出的组合数量将是无限的，而且其中大多数绝对没有兴趣。创造恰恰在于不进行无用的组合，而在于做出有用的、但只是少数的组合。发明是洞察力、选择。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("How to make this choice I have before explained; the mathematical facts worthy of being studied are those which, by their analogy with other facts, are capable of leading us to the knowledge of a mathematical law just as experimental facts lead us to the knowledge of a physical law. They are those which reveal to us unsuspected kinship between other facts, long known, but wrongly believed to be strangers to one another.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("如何做出这个选择我之前已经解释过了；值得研究的数学事实是那些通过与其他事实的类比，能够引导我们了解数学定律的知识，就像实验事实引导我们了解物理定律一样。它们向我们揭示了其他事实之间不可怀疑的亲缘关系，这些事实早已为人所知，但却被错误地认为彼此陌生。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("Among chosen combinations the most fertile will often be those formed of elements drawn from domains which are far apart. Not that I mean as sufficing for invention the bringing together of objects as disparate as possible; most combinations so formed would be entirely sterile. But certain among them, very rare, are the most fruitful of all.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("在选定的组合中，最丰富的组合通常是由来自相距较远的域的元素组成的组合。我的意思并不是说，将尽可能不同的物体组合在一起就足以实现发明；而是说，发明的目的在于将不同的物体尽可能地组合在一起。如此形成的大多数组合将是完全无菌的。但其中某些非常罕见，但却是最富有成效的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("To invent, I have said, is to choose; but the word is perhaps not wholly exact. It makes one think of a purchaser before whom are displayed a large number of samples, and who examines them, one after the other, to make a choice. Here the samples would be so numerous that a whole lifetime would not suffice to examine them. This is not the actual state of things. The sterile combinations do not even present themselves to the mind of the inventor. Never in the field of his consciousness do combinations appear that are not really useful, except some that he rejects but which have to some extent the characteristics of useful combinations. All goes on as if the inventor were an examiner for the second degree who would only have to question the candidates who had passed a previous examination.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("我说过，发明就是选择；发明就是选择。但这个词或许并不完全准确。它让人想起一位购买者，面前展示着大量样品，他一个接一个地检查这些样品，然后做出选择。这里的样本数量如此之多，以至于一生都不足以检查它们。这不是事情的实际情况。无菌组合甚至没有出现在发明人的脑海中。在他的意识领域中，从来没有出现过没有真正有用的组合，除了一些他拒绝但在某种程度上具有有用组合特征的组合。一切都在继续，就好像发明人是第二学位的考官，他只需要向已经通过先前考试的考生提出问题。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("But what I have hitherto said is what may be observed or inferred in reading the writings of the geometers, reading reflectively.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("但到目前为止，我所说的是在阅读几何学家的著作、反思性阅读时可以观察到或推断出的东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("It is time to penetrate deeper and to see what goes on in the very soul of the mathematician. For this, I believe, I can do best by recalling memories of my own. But I shall limit myself to telling how I wrote my first memoir on Fuchsian functions. I beg the reader's pardon; I am about to use some technical expressions, but they need not frighten him, for he is not obliged to understand them. I shall say, for example, that I have found the demonstration of such a theorem under such circumstances. This theorem will have a barbarous name, unfamiliar to many, but that is unimportant; what is of interest for the psychologist is not the theorem but the circumstances.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("是时候更深入地了解数学家的灵魂了。为此，我相信，我可以通过回忆自己的记忆来做到最好。但我将仅限于讲述我是如何写下第一本关于 Fuchsian 函数的回忆录的。恳请读者原谅；我将要使用一些技术表达，但它们不必吓到他，因为他没有义务理解它们。例如，我会说，我在这种情况下找到了这样一个定理的证明。这个定理有一个野蛮的名字，很多人都不熟悉，但这并不重要。心理学家感兴趣的不是定理，而是环境。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("For fifteen days I strove to prove that there could not be any functions like those I have since called Fuchsian functions. I was then very ignorant; every day I seated myself at my work table, stayed an hour or two, tried a great number of combinations and reached no results. One evening, contrary to my custom, I drank black coffee and could not sleep. Ideas rose in crowds; I felt them collide until pairs interlocked, so to speak, making a stable combination. By the next morning I had established the existence of a class of Fuchsian functions, those which come from the hypergeometric series; I had only to write out the results, which took but a few hours.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("用了十五天的时间，我努力证明不可能有任何像我后来称为 Fuchsian 函数的函数。那时我很无知；每天我坐在工作台前，呆上一两个小时，尝试了很多种组合，但都没有结果。一天晚上，我一反常态，喝了黑咖啡，睡不着。想法在人群中涌现。我感觉到它们相互碰撞，直到成对相扣，可以说，形成了一个稳定的组合。到第二天早上，我已经确定了一类 Fuchsian 函数的存在，它们来自超几何级数；我只需要把结果写出来，只花了几个小时。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("Then I wanted to represent these functions by the quotient of two series; this idea was perfectly conscious and deliberate, the analogy with elliptic functions guided me. I asked myself what properties these series must have if they existed, and I succeeded without difficulty in forming the series I have called theta-Fuchsian.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("然后我想用两个级数的商来表示这些函数；这个想法是完全有意识和深思熟虑的，与椭圆函数的类比引导了我。我问自己，如果这些级数存在的话，它们必须具有什么属性，然后我毫无困难地成功地形成了我称之为 theta-Fuchsian 的级数。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("Just at this time I left Caen, where I was then living, to go on a geologic excursion under the auspices of the school of mines. The changes of travel made me forget my mathematical work. Having reached Coutances, we entered an omnibus to go some place or other. At the moment when I put my foot on the step the idea came to me, without anything in my former thoughts seeming to have paved the way for it, that the transformations I had used to define the Fuchsian functions were identical with those of non-Euclidean geometry. I did not verify the idea; I should not have had time, as, upon taking my seat in the omnibus, I went on with a conversation already commenced, but I felt a perfect certainty. On my return to Caen, for conscience' sake I verified the result at my leisure.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("就在这个时候，我离开了当时居住的卡昂，在矿业学校的赞助下进行了一次地质考察。旅行的改变让我忘记了我的数学工作。到达库坦斯后，我们上了一辆公共汽车去某个地方。当我的脚踏上台阶的那一刻，我突然想到，我用来定义福克斯函数的变换与非欧几里得几何的变换是相同的，而我以前的想法似乎没有为它铺平道路。我没有验证这个想法；我本不应该有时间的，因为在公共汽车上坐下后，我继续进行已经开始的谈话，但我感到完全确定。返回卡昂后，出于良心，我在闲暇时验证了结果。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("Then I turned my attention to the study of some arithmetical questions apparently without much success and without a suspicion of any connection with my preceding researches. Disgusted with my failure, I went to spend a few days at the seaside, and thought of something else. One morning, walking on the bluff, the idea came to me, with just the same characteristics of brevity, suddenness and immediate certainty, that the arithmetic transformations of indeterminate ternary quadratic forms were identical with those of non-Euclidean geometry.")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("然后我把注意力转向一些算术问题的研究，显然没有取得多大成功，也没有怀疑与我之前的研究有任何联系。我对自己的失败感到厌恶，于是去海边呆了几天，并想到了别的事情。一天早上，在悬崖上行走时，我突然想到了一个想法，即不定三元二次形式的算术变换与非欧几里得几何的算术变换是相同的，具有同样的简洁、突然和直接确定性的特点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("Returned to Caen, I meditated on this result and deduced the consequences. The example of quadratic forms showed me that there were Fuchsian groups other than those corresponding to the hypergeometric series; I saw that I could apply to them the theory of theta-Fuchsian series and that consequently there existed Fuchsian functions other than those from the hypergeometric series, the ones I then knew. Naturally I set myself to form all these functions. I made a systematic attack upon them and carried all the outworks, one after another. There was one however that still held out, whose fall would involve that of the whole place. But all my efforts only served at first the better to show me the difficulty, which indeed was something. All this work was perfectly conscious.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("回到卡昂后，我思考了这个结果并推断出后果。二次形式的例子告诉我，除了对应于超几何级数的群之外，还存在 Fuchsian 群；我发现我可以将 theta-Fuchsian 级数理论应用于它们，因此除了我当时知道的超几何级数之外，还存在 Fuchsian 函数。我自然而然地让自己形成了所有这些功能。我对他们进行了系统性的攻击，并一个接一个地完成了所有的外墙工事。然而，有一个人仍然坚持了下来，他的倒下将涉及整个地方。但我所有的努力一开始只是为了更好地向我展示困难，这确实是一件很困难的事情。所有这些工作都是完全有意识的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("Thereupon I left for Mont-Valérien, where I was to go through my military service; so I was very differently occupied. One day, going along the street, the solution of the difficulty which had stopped me suddenly appeared to me. I did not try to go deep into it immediately, and only after my service did I again take up the question. I had all the elements and had only to arrange them and put them together. So I wrote out my final memoir at a single stroke and without difficulty.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("于是我前往蒙瓦莱里安，在那里服兵役。所以我的工作非常不同。有一天，沿着街道走着，一直困扰着我的难题的解决办法突然出现在我眼前。我没有立即深入探讨，直到礼拜结束后我才再次提出这个问题。我拥有所有元素，只需将它们排列并组合在一起即可。于是我毫不费力地一笔画出了我最后的回忆录。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("I shall limit myself to this single example; it is useless to multiply them. In regard to my other researches I would have to say analogous things, and the observations of other mathematicians given in L'enseignement mathématique would only confirm them.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("我将仅限于这个例子；乘以它们是没有用的。关于我的其他研究，我不得不说类似的事情，而《数学知识》中其他数学家的观察只能证实这些。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("Most striking at first is this appearance of sudden illumination, a manifest sign of long, unconscious prior work. The rôle of this unconscious work in mathematical invention appears to me incontestable, and traces of it would be found in other cases where it is less evident. Often when one works at a hard question, nothing good is accomplished at the first attack. Then one takes a rest, longer or shorter, and sits down anew to the work. During the first half-hour, as before, nothing is found, and then all of a sudden the decisive idea presents itself to the mind. It might be said that the conscious work has been more fruitful because it has been interrupted and the rest has given back to the mind its force and freshness. But it is more probable that this rest has been filled out with unconscious work and that the result of this work has afterward revealed itself to the geometer just as in the cases I have cited; only the revelation, instead of coming during a walk or a journey, has happened during a period of conscious work, but independently of this work which plays at most a rôle of excitant, as if it were the goad stimulating the results already reached during rest, but remaining unconscious, to assume the conscious form.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("一开始最引人注目的是这种突然的启发，这是长期、无意识的先前工作的明显标志。在我看来，这种无意识的工作在数学发明中的作用是无可争议的，而且在其他不太明显的情况下也会发现它的踪迹。通常，当一个人解决一个难题时，第一次攻击并不会取得任何好处。然后，人们休息一段时间，或长或短，然后重新坐下来工作。在前半个小时里，和以前一样，什么也没有发现，然后突然间，决定性的想法出现在脑海中。可以说，有意识的工作更加富有成果，因为它被打断了，而其余的工作又给心灵带来了力量和新鲜感。但更有可能的是，这个休息已经被无意识的工作所填补，并且这项工作的结果后来向几何学家显现出来，就像我所引用的例子一样；只是启示不是在散步或旅行中出现，而是在一段有意识的工作期间发生，但独立于该工作，最多起着兴奋的作用，就好像它是刺激在休息时已经达到的结果一样，但保持无意识，呈现有意识的形式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("There is another remark to be made about the conditions of this unconscious work: it is possible, and of a certainty it is only fruitful, if it is on the one hand preceded and on the other hand followed by a period of conscious work. These sudden inspirations (and the examples already cited sufficiently prove this) never happen except after some days of voluntary effort which has appeared absolutely fruitless and whence nothing good seems to have come, where the way taken seems totally astray. These efforts then have not been as sterile as one thinks; they have set agoing the unconscious machine and without them it would not have moved and would have produced nothing.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("关于这种无意识工作的条件，还有一点要说的是：它是可能的，并且可以肯定的是，只有在它之前和之后都有一段有意识的工作的情况下，它才会富有成果。这些突然的灵感（已经引用的例子足以证明这一点）永远不会发生，除非经过几天的自愿努力，这种努力似乎完全没有结果，似乎没有什么好的结果，所采取的方式似乎完全误入歧途。这些努力并不像人们想象的那样毫无结果。它们已经启动了无意识的机器，没有它们，它就不会移动，也不会产生任何东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("The need for the second period of conscious work, after the inspiration, is still easier to understand. It is necessary to put in shape the results of this inspiration, to deduce from them the immediate consequences, to arrange them, to word the demonstrations, but above all is verification necessary. I have spoken of the feeling of absolute certitude accompanying the inspiration; in the cases cited this feeling was no deceiver, nor is it usually. But do not think this a rule without exception; often this feeling deceives us without being any the less vivid, and we only find it out when we seek to put on foot the demonstration. I have especially noticed this fact in regard to ideas coming to me in the morning or evening in bed while in a semi-hypnagogic state.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("灵感之后第二阶段有意识工作的需要，还是比较容易理解的。有必要将这种灵感的结果具体化，从中推断出直接的后果，安排它们，表达示范，但最重要的是验证是必要的。我已经谈到了伴随着灵感而来的绝对确定感。在所引用的案例中，这种感觉并不是骗人的，通常也不是。但不要认为这是无一例外的规则；这种感觉常常欺骗我们，但又不那么生动，只有当我们试图亲自示范时，我们才会发现它。我特别注意到这一事实，即早上或晚上在床上处于半催眠状态时出现的想法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("Such are the realities; now for the thoughts they force upon us. The unconscious, or, as we say, the subliminal self plays an important rôle in mathematical creation; this follows from what we have said. But usually the subliminal self is considered as purely automatic. Now we have seen that mathematical work is not simply mechanical, that it could not be done by a machine, however perfect. It is not merely a question of applying rules, of making the most combinations possible according to certain fixed laws. The combinations so obtained would be exceedingly numerous, useless and cumbersome. The true work of the inventor consists in choosing among these combinations so as to eliminate the useless ones or rather to avoid the trouble of making them, and the rules which must guide this choice are extremely fine and delicate. It is almost impossible to state them precisely; they are felt rather than formulated. Under these conditions, how imagine a sieve capable of applying them mechanically?")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("这就是现实；现在谈谈他们强加给我们的想法。无意识，或者我们所说的潜意识自我在数学创造中发挥着重要作用。这是根据我们所说的得出的。但通常潜意识自我被认为是纯粹自动的。现在我们已经看到，数学工作不仅仅是机械的，它不能由机器完成，无论机器多么完美。这不仅仅是一个应用规则、根据某些固定法则使尽可能多的组合成为可能的问题。如此获得的组合将会非常多、无用且麻烦。发明人的真正工作在于在这些组合中进行选择，以消除无用的组合，或者更确切地说，避免制造它们的麻烦，并且指导这种选择的规则必须是极其精细和微妙的。准确地表述它们几乎是不可能的；它们是被感觉出来的，而不是被表述出来的。在这些条件下，如何想象能够机械地应用它们的筛子？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("A first hypothesis now presents itself: the subliminal self is in no way inferior to the conscious self; it is not purely automatic; it is capable of discernment; it has tact, delicacy; it knows how to choose, to divine. What do I say? It knows better how to divine than the conscious self, since it succeeds where that has failed. In a word, is not the subliminal self superior to the conscious self? You recognize the full importance of this question. Boutroux in a recent lecture has shown how it came up on a very different occasion, and what consequences would follow an affirmative answer. (See also, by the same author, Science et Religion, pp. 313 ff.)")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("现在出现了第一个假设：潜意识的自我绝不逊色于有意识的自我；它不是纯粹自动的；它有辨别能力；它机智、细腻；它知道如何选择、如何占卜。我说什么？它比有意识的自我更懂得如何占卜，因为它在失败的地方取得了成功。总而言之，潜意识的自我不是比意识的自我更优越吗？您认识到这个问题的重要性。布特鲁在最近的一次讲座中展示了它是如何在一个非常不同的场合出现的，以及肯定的答案会带来什么后果。 （另见同一作者的《科学与宗教》，第 313 页及以下）")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("Is this affirmative answer forced upon us by the facts I have just given? I confess that, for my part, I should hate to accept it. Reexamine the facts then and see if they are not compatible with another explanation.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("这个肯定的答案是我刚才给出的事实强加给我们的吗？我承认，就我而言，我应该不愿意接受它。然后重新审视事实，看看它们是否与其他解释不相容。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("It is certain that the combinations which present themselves to the mind in a sort of sudden illumination, after an unconscious working somewhat prolonged, are generally useful and fertile combinations, which seem the result of a first impression. Does it follow that the subliminal self, having divined by a delicate intuition that these combinations would be useful, has formed only these, or has it rather formed many others which were lacking in interest and have remained unconscious?")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("可以肯定的是，在经过一段时间的无意识工作之后，以一种突然的启发而呈现在头脑中的组合通常是有用且丰富的组合，这似乎是第一印象的结果。这是否表明，潜意识自我通过微妙的直觉预见到这些组合将是有用的，因此只形成了这些组合，或者它形成了许多其他缺乏兴趣并保持无意识的组合？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("In this second way of looking at it, all the combinations would be formed in consequence of the automatism of the subliminal self, but only the interesting ones would break into the domain of consciousness. And this is still very mysterious. What is the cause that, among the thousand products of our unconscious activity, some are called to pass the threshold, while others remain below? Is it a simple chance which confers this privilege? Evidently not; among all the stimuli of our senses, for example, only the most intense fix our attention, unless it has been drawn to them by other causes. More generally the privileged unconscious phenomena, those susceptible of becoming conscious, are those which, directly or indirectly, affect most profoundly our emotional sensibility.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("从第二种角度来看，所有的组合都是由于潜意识自我的自动性而形成的，但只有有趣的组合才会进入意识领域。而这仍然是非常神秘的。是什么原因导致在我们无意识活动的数千种产物中，有些被要求通过门槛，而另一些则仍然低于门槛？是不是一个简单的机会就赋予了这种特权？显然不是；例如，在我们所有的感官刺激中，只有最强烈的刺激才会引起我们的注意，除非是由于其他原因而引起的。更一般地说，那些容易成为意识的特权无意识现象是那些直接或间接地最深刻地影响我们的情感感受力的现象。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("It may be surprising to see emotional sensibility invoked à propos of mathematical demonstrations which, it would seem, can interest only the intellect. This would be to forget the feeling of mathematical beauty, of the harmony of numbers and forms, of geometric elegance. This is a true esthetic feeling that all real mathematicians know, and surely it belongs to emotional sensibility.")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("看到情感敏感性被数学论证的提议所调用，这可能会令人惊讶，而数学论证似乎只能引起智力的兴趣。这将让人忘记数学之美、数字与形式的和谐以及几何优雅的感觉。这是所有真正的数学家都知道的真正的审美感受，当然它属于情感感受。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("Now, what are the mathematic entities to which we attribute this character of beauty and elegance, and which are capable of developing in us a sort of esthetic emotion? They are those whose elements are harmoniously disposed so that the mind without effort can embrace their totality while realizing the details. This harmony is at once a satisfaction of our esthetic needs and an aid to the mind, sustaining and guiding; And at the same time, in putting under our eyes a well-ordered whole, it makes us foresee a mathematical law. Now, as we have said above, the only mathematical facts worthy of fixing our attention and capable of being useful are those which can teach us a mathematical law. So that we reach the following conclusion: The useful combinations are precisely the most beautiful, I mean those best able to charm this special sensibility that all mathematicians know, but of which the profane are so ignorant as often to be tempted to smile at it.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("现在，我们赋予这种美丽和优雅的特征，并且能够在我们身上发展出一种审美情感的数学实体是什么？它们的元素和谐地排列在一起，这样头脑就可以毫不费力地拥抱它们的整体，同时意识到细节。这种和谐既能满足我们的审美需求，又能帮助我们的心灵，维持和引导我们的心灵。同时，在我们眼前看到一个有序的整体，它使我们预见到了数学定律。现在，正如我们上面所说，唯一值得我们关注并能够发挥作用的数学事实是那些能够教给我们数学定律的事实。因此，我们得出以下结论：有用的组合恰恰是最美丽的，我的意思是那些最能吸引所有数学家都知道的特殊情感的组合，但世俗的人却对此一无所知，常常想对它微笑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("What happens then? Among the great numbers of combinations blindly formed by the subliminal self, almost all are without interest and without utility; but just for that reason they are also without effect upon the esthetic sensibility. Consciousness will never know them; only certain ones are harmonious, and, consequently, at once useful and beautiful. They will be capable of touching this special sensibility of the geometer of which I have just spoken, and which, once aroused, will call our attention to them, and thus give them occasion to become conscious.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("然后会发生什么？在潜意识自我盲目形成的大量组合中，几乎都是无趣、无用的；但正因为如此，它们也不影响审美感受。意识永远不会认识他们；只有某些东西是和谐的，因此既有用又美观。他们将能够触及我刚才谈到的几何学家的这种特殊情感，这种情感一旦被唤醒，就会引起我们对他们的注意，从而使他们有机会变得有意识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("This is only a hypothesis, and yet here is an observation which may confirm it: when a sudden illumination seizes upon the mind of the mathematician, it usually happens that it does not deceive him, but it also sometimes happens, as I have said, that it does not stand the test of verification; well, we almost always notice that this false idea, had it been true, would have gratified our natural feeling for mathematical elegance.")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("这只是一个假设，但这里有一个观察可以证实它：当数学家的头脑突然灵光乍现时，通常情况下它不会欺骗他，但有时，正如我已经说过的，它也经不起验证的检验。好吧，我们几乎总是注意到，这个错误的想法，如果它是真的，将会满足我们对数学优雅的自然感觉。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("Thus it is this special esthetic sensibility which plays the rôle of the delicate sieve of which I spoke, and that sufficiently explains why the one lacking it will never be a real creator.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("因此，正是这种特殊的审美情感发挥了我所说的精致筛子的作用，并充分解释了为什么缺乏它的人永远不会成为真正的创造者。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("Yet all the difficulties have not disappeared. The conscious self is narrowly limited, and as for the subliminal self we know not its limitations, and this is why we are not too reluctant in supposing that it has been able in a short time to make more different combinations than the whole life of a conscious being could encompass. Yet these limitations exist. Is it likely that it is able to form all the possible combinations, whose number would frighten the imagination? Nevertheless that would seem necessary, because if it produces only a small part of these combinations, and if it makes them at random, there would be small chance that the good, the one we should choose, would be found among them.")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("然而所有的困难并没有消失。有意识的自我是有限的，至于潜意识的自我，我们不知道它的局限性，这就是为什么我们不太愿意假设它能够在短时间内做出比有意识存在的整个生命所能包含的更多不同的组合。然而这些限制仍然存在。是否能够组合出所有可能的组合，其数量之多令人难以想象？尽管如此，这似乎是必要的，因为如果它只产生这些组合中的一小部分，并且如果它是随机组合的，那么在其中找到我们应该选择的善的机会就很小。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("Perhaps we ought to seek the explanation in that preliminary period of conscious work which always precedes all fruitful unconscious labor. Permit me a rough comparison. Figure the future elements of our combinations as something like the hooked atoms of Epicurus. During the complete repose of the mind, these atoms are motionless, they are, so to speak, hooked to the wall; so this complete rest may be indefinitely prolonged without the atoms meeting, and consequently without any combination between them.")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("也许我们应该在有意识工作的初始阶段寻求解释，它总是先于所有富有成效的无意识劳动。请允许我粗略地比较一下。将我们组合的未来元素想象成伊壁鸠鲁的钩状原子。当心灵完全休息时，这些原子一动不动，可以说，它们挂在墙上；因此，这种完全静止可以无限期地延长，而原子不会相遇，因此它们之间不会有任何结合。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("On the other hand, during a period of apparent rest and unconscious work, certain of them are detached from the wall and put in motion. They flash in every direction through the space (I was about to say the room) where they are enclosed, as would, for example, a swarm of gnats or, if you prefer a more learned comparison, like the molecules of gas in the kinematic theory of gases. Then their mutual impacts may produce new combinations.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("另一方面，在明显的休息和无意识的工作期间，其中某些人会脱离墙壁并开始运动。它们在封闭的空间（我正要说房间）中向各个方向闪烁，例如，一群蚊子，或者，如果你喜欢更深入的比较，就像气体运动理论中的气体分子一样。那么它们的相互影响可能会产生新的组合。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("What is the rôle of the preliminary conscious work? It is evidently to mobilize certain of these atoms, to unhook them from the wall and put them in swing. We think we have done no good, because we have moved these elements a thousand different ways in seeking to assemble them, and have found no satisfactory aggregate. But, after this shaking up imposed upon them by our will, these atoms do not return to their primitive rest. They freely continue their dance.")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("初步意识工作的作用是什么？显然是为了调动这些原子中的某些原子，将它们从墙上解下来并让它们摇摆起来。我们认为我们没有做任何好事，因为我们以一千种不同的方式移动这些元素以寻求组装它们，但没有找到令人满意的聚合体。但是，在我们的意志强加给它们的震动之后，这些原子并没有回到它们原始的静止状态。他们自由自在地继续跳舞。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("Now, our will did not choose them at random; it pursued a perfectly determined aim. The mobilized atoms are therefore not any atoms whatsoever; they are those from which we might reasonably expect the desired solution. Then the mobilized atoms undergo impacts which make them enter into combinations among themselves or with other atoms at rest which they struck against in their course. Again I beg pardon, my comparison is very rough, but I scarcely know how otherwise to make my thought understood.")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("现在，我们的意志并不是随机选择他们的；而是我们的意志选择了他们。它追求一个完全确定的目标。因此，被动员的原子并不是任何原子；而是原子。我们可以合理地期望从中得到所需的解决方案。然后，移动的原子受到撞击，使它们彼此结合或与它们在运动过程中撞击的其他静止原子结合。再次请原谅，我的比较非常粗略，但我几乎不知道如何才能让我的想法被理解。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("However it may be, the only combinations that have a chance of forming are those where at least one of the elements is one of those atoms freely chosen by our will. Now, it is evidently among these that is found what I called the good combination. Perhaps this is a way of lessening the paradoxical in the original hypothesis.")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("不管怎样，唯一有机会形成的组合是其中至少一种元素是由我们的意志自由选择的原子之一的组合。现在，显然正是在这些之中找到了我所说的良好组合。也许这是减少原始假设中的矛盾的一种方式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S045")[#text("Another observation. It never happens that the unconscious work gives us the result of a somewhat long calculation all made, where we have only to apply fixed rules. We might think the wholly automatic subliminal self particularly apt for this sort of work, which is in a way exclusively mechanical. It seems that thinking in the evening upon the factors of a multiplication we might hope to find the product ready made upon our awakening, or again that an algebraic calculation, for example a verification, would be made unconsciously. Nothing of the sort, as observation proves. All one may hope from these inspirations, fruits of unconscious work, is a point of departure for such calculations. As for the calculations themselves, they must be made in the second period of conscious work, that which follows the inspiration, that in which one verifies the results of this inspiration and deduces their consequences. The rules of these calculations are strict and complicated. They require discipline, attention, will, and therefore consciousness. In the subliminal self, on the contrary, reigns what I should call liberty, if we might give this name to the simple absence of discipline and to the disorder born of chance. Only, this disorder itself permits unexpected combinations.")]

#zh(id: "S045")[#text("另一个观察。无意识的工作从来不会给我们带来一个相当长的计算结果，而我们只需要应用固定的规则。我们可能认为完全自动的潜意识自我特别适合这种工作，在某种程度上完全是机械性的。看来，在晚上思考乘法因子时，我们可能希望在醒来时找到现成的乘积，或者再次无意识地进行代数计算，例如验证。正如观察所证明的那样，事实并非如此。人们可能希望从这些灵感、无意识工作的成果中得到的一切，都是这种计算的出发点。至于计算本身，必须在有意识工作的第二阶段进行，即灵感之后的阶段，即验证这一灵感的结果并推断其后果的阶段。这些计算的规则严格而复杂。它们需要纪律、注意力、意志，因此也需要意识。相反，在潜意识自我中，统治着我所说的自由，如果我们可以用这个名字来形容单纯的缺乏纪律和偶然产生的混乱的话。只是，这种疾病本身允许意想不到的组合。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("I shall make a last remark: when above I made certain personal observations, I spoke of a night of excitement when I worked in spite of myself. Such cases are frequent, and it is not necessary that the abnormal cerebral activity be caused by a physical excitant as in that I mentioned. It seems, in such cases, that one is present at his own unconscious work, made partially perceptible to the over-excited consciousness, yet without having changed its nature. Then we vaguely comprehend what distinguishes the two mechanisms or, if you wish, the working methods of the two egos. And the psychologic observations I have been able thus to make seem to me to confirm in their general outlines the views I have given.")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("我最后要说的是：当我在上面发表某些个人观察时，我谈到了我不由自主地工作时的一个兴奋之夜。这种情况是经常发生的，大脑活动异常并不一定是我所说的由身体刺激引起的。在这种情况下，似乎一个人正处于自己无意识的工作中，过度兴奋的意识部分地可以察觉到，但其本质却没有改变。然后我们就模糊地理解了这两种机制的区别，或者，如果你愿意的话，理解了两种自我的工作方法的区别。在我看来，我所进行的心理观察在总体轮廓上证实了我所给出的观点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("Surely they have need of it, for they are and remain in spite of all very hypothetical: the interest of the questions is so great that I do not repent of having submitted them to the reader.")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("当然，他们需要它，因为尽管一切都是假设性的，但它们仍然是并且仍然是：这些问题的兴趣是如此之大，以至于我不会后悔将它们提交给读者。")]
